# P. SECURITY AUDIT

## PM-AUDIT-001 — Audit Record

Contains audit identity, Host/security-state context, actor/client/principal, decision reference, event type, authority epoch, classification, integrity metadata, retention, and contract/schema versions.

## PM-AUDIT-002 — Mandatory Audit Ordering

If policy requires audit before protected disclosure/effect, the minimum audit record is durable before release/effect.

## PM-AUDIT-003 — Denied Request Logging Failure

A denied request remains denied even if recording the denial later fails. Audit subsystem failure may trigger fault/throttle/alert; it never grants the request.

## PM-AUDIT-004 — Audit Integrity

Security-grade audit integrity uses keyed/signature/tamper-evident mechanisms when required. Plain adjacent hashes are insufficient for offline-tamper claims.

## PM-AUDIT-005 — Rollback-Evident Audit

Policies needing rollback detection use an anchored rollback-evident durability class.

## PM-AUDIT-006 — Audit Query Continuation

Audit/history cursors bind Host, source, principal, DisclosureContext, schema, and expiry. Elevated cursors cannot be replayed after the context ends.

## PM-AUDIT-007 — Historical Verification

Integrity algorithm/key epoch is retained so old records remain verifiable after key rotation; old keys cannot sign new current records.

