# R. HOST ACTION MODEL

## PM-ACTION-001 — Host Action Starts at Durable Admission

Pre-admission denial is not a Host Action.

## PM-ACTION-002 — Portable Lifecycle Axes

At minimum distinguish durable admission, dispatch, commit/effect boundary, reconciliation state, and terminal/nonterminal outcome.

Portable outcomes include `SUCCEEDED`, `FAILED`, `PARTIAL`, `NO_CHANGE`, `CANCELLED`, `UNKNOWN_FINAL_STATE` or equivalent.

## PM-ACTION-003 — Desired-State Preference

Prefer absolute desired-state effects such as STOP/SUSPEND/SET_VALUE over replay-ambiguous toggles/increments.

## PM-ACTION-004 — No Silent Escalation

Graceful terminate never silently becomes force-stop.

## PM-ACTION-005 — Cancellation Is Not Rollback

Cancellation is pre-commit where supported. Post-commit compensation/revert is a new effect/action.

## PM-ACTION-006 — Unknown Outcome Is a Retry Fence

Reconcile first; do not blindly resubmit.

## PM-ACTION-007 — Mutation Conflict Domain

Conflicting effects from Manager/Terminal/system authorities serialize, use an authoritative mutation lease/fence, or return conflict.

## PM-ACTION-008 — Composite Action Phases

Every phase is evidenced separately. Revalidate target/definition/Provider/protection between phases.

## PM-ACTION-009 — Expected Disconnect Scopes

Explicit scopes include Manager client, Core control channel, graphical session, Provider link, remote Host link, Host reboot, and Host power, each with its own reconciliation rule.

## PM-ACTION-010 — Read-After-Write Reconciliation

Where mechanically available, successful action result exposes resulting revision/state and event/projection continuation information for authoritative reconciliation.

## PM-ACTION-011 — Self-Target Safety

Manager renderer/broker self-stop/suspend/starvation is protection-assessed. Self-stop requires expected-disconnect semantics. Self-suspend/starvation is unavailable without another authorized recovery/control path.

## PM-ACTION-012 — Stable UI Targeting

Actions bind stable object/target-set identity, never table row position.

## PM-ACTION-013 — Historical/Paused View Safety

Acting from paused/history/tombstone views re-resolves the current live target and reruns required preflight/authorization.

## PM-ACTION-014 — Multi-Target Semantics

Every bulk/tree effect declares `ALL_OR_NONE`, `PARTITIONED`, or another explicit semantic. Manager never silently drops failed members and continues on a changed set without recomputing the exact set digest and aggregate safety.

## PM-ACTION-015 — No Implied Atomicity

Unless an authoritative backend explicitly guarantees all-or-none, multi-target effects may partially commit and must expose per-target outcomes/unknowns.

## PM-ACTION-016 — Dynamic Target Bounds

Expanding target discovery is bounded by cardinality/work/time. Exceeding the bound yields truthful PARTIAL/INCOMPLETE rather than unbounded Host work.

## PM-POST-001 — PostconditionPredicate

Reconciliable effects declare a typed predicate over a defined target/state/revision domain with results `SATISFIED`, `NOT_SATISFIED`, `UNKNOWN`.

## PM-POST-002 — Postcondition Is Not Causality

Satisfied postcondition does not prove the action caused it. Not-satisfied later does not prove the action never committed.

## PM-REPLAY-001 — Replay/Reconciliation Classes

Every effect descriptor declares a class such as `IDEMPOTENT_STATE_SET`, `QUERYABLE_BY_ACTION_ID`, `POSTCONDITION_RECONCILABLE`, `DURABLE_HANDOFF_RECONCILABLE`, `ONE_SHOT_UNRECONCILABLE` or equivalent.

